config MESHTASTIC_STACK_PROTECTION
	bool "Best stack-overflow protection this SoC can offer"
	default y
	select HW_STACK_PROTECTION if ARCH_HAS_STACK_PROTECTION
	select STACK_SENTINEL if !ARCH_HAS_STACK_PROTECTION && !MPU_STACK_GUARD
	select STACK_USAGE
	select INIT_STACKS
	help
	  Every image gets stack-overflow detection, and the mechanism is chosen
	  per SoC rather than assumed.

	  This exists because the naive version is WRONG, and was briefly shipped
	  that way on 2026-08-30: writing CONFIG_STACK_SENTINEL=y into prj.conf
	  looks like it makes the floor uniform, and on an nRF52840 Kconfig
	  silently discards it. STACK_SENTINEL carries `depends on
	  !MPU_STACK_GUARD`, so on any board with an MPU it is refused -- with a
	  warning nobody reads -- and the config that appears to have raised the
	  floor everywhere has in fact raised it nowhere new.

	  The two SoCs on this bench sit on opposite sides of that:

	    nRF52840   ARCH_HAS_STACK_PROTECTION=y, so HW_STACK_PROTECTION gives a
	               real MPU guard REGION. The offending access faults
	               immediately, at the instruction that made it. The sentinel
	               is redundant here and correctly excluded.

	    ESP32-S3   declares no ARCH_HAS_STACK_PROTECTION at all, so there is no
	               guard to have and STACK_SENTINEL is the ONLY option -- a
	               magic word checked at context switch, which a fast deep
	               recursion can smash past and wedge the machine before any
	               switch happens. Measured: `meshtastic crashtest stack` on a
	               V4 produced a bare `cause 0x10 WATCHDOG` with no fatal, no
	               coredump and nothing in the log ring.

	  So the ESP32 boards have the WEAKER guarantee, and it is not fixable in
	  config -- which is the argument for watching stack headroom shrink
	  rather than waiting for an overflow to be detected.

	  STACK_USAGE and INIT_STACKS come along because without the fill pattern
	  the thread high-water numbers have nothing to measure against, and those
	  numbers are the input to the watching.
